<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Namur</h1><p class="st-meta">981 CE – 1795 CE (approx.) · capital Namur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A compact county at the meeting of the Sambre and Meuse, built around a rock fortress that later became one of the most fought-over strongholds in Europe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Namur emerged in the 10th century from the old district of Lomme, when local counts built their power around the castle on the rock above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambre">Sambre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> confluence. Albert I, ruling around 981, is often treated as the first well-documented count, and his family held the county for two centuries.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Namur was a small Walloon county of river trade, forests and ironworks, an imperial fief squeezed between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Liège</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">Hainaut</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Brabant</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>. Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-blind">Henry the Blind</a> also held Luxembourg in the 12th century. From 1263 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-dampierre">Dampierre</a> counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-flanders">Flanders</a> ruled it, and Namur's citadel grew into a major fortress.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The bankrupt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-count-of-namur">John III</a> sold Namur to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a> in 1421, and it joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/burgundian-state">Burgundian State</a>, then the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/habsburg-netherlands">Habsburg Netherlands</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> took the citadel in 1692 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iii-of-england">William III</a> retook it in 1695. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed the county in 1795; it is now a Belgian province.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-namur.jpg" alt="County of Namur" width="320" height="413"><figcaption>Image: Watisfictie · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Low_Countries_Locator_Namur.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>981 CE – 1795 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Namur</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Albert I of Namur, Henry the Blind, John III of Namur</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French and Walloon</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Namur</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1388&amp;focus=county-of-namur" aria-label="County of Namur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4791 425 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Namur lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5111 681L5229 694M5111 681L5118 774M5111 681L5102 664M5111 681L5099 670M5111 681L5099 670"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5118 774h0M5102 664h0M5099 670h0M5099 670h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5111 681h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5111 681h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Namur. <a href="/atlas?year=1388&amp;focus=county-of-namur">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

